## QueryParameter attribute

```php
<?php
// api/src/Resource/Product.php
namespace App\Resource;

use ApiPlatform\Doctrine\Orm\Filter\ComparisonFilter;
use ApiPlatform\Doctrine\Orm\Filter\ExactFilter;
use ApiPlatform\Metadata\ApiResource;
use ApiPlatform\Metadata\GetCollection;
use ApiPlatform\Metadata\QueryParameter;

#[ApiResource(operations: [
    new GetCollection(
        parameters: [
            'name' => new QueryParameter(
                filter: ExactFilter::class,   // filter: class name or instance
                property: 'name',             // resource property to map to
                required: false,              // required: bool
                description: 'Filter by name' // description: shown in docs
            ),
        ]
    ),
])]
class Product {}
```

`QueryParameter` is in `ApiPlatform\Metadata`. Other supported arguments include `key`, `properties`, `constraints`, `schema`, `castToNativeType`, `castToArray`, `openApi`, `hydra`, `security`, `provider`, and `extraProperties`. `HeaderParameter` takes the same arguments for HTTP headers.

## Custom filter keys such as `filter[minPrice]`

The page doesn't show this exact case. It does show bracketed keys such as `search[:property]`, so the key can contain brackets. The example below combines that with `ComparisonFilter`:

```php
<?php
// api/src/Resource/Product.php
namespace App\Resource;

use ApiPlatform\Doctrine\Orm\Filter\ComparisonFilter;
use ApiPlatform\Doctrine\Orm\Filter\ExactFilter;
use ApiPlatform\Metadata\ApiResource;
use ApiPlatform\Metadata\GetCollection;
use ApiPlatform\Metadata\QueryParameter;

#[ApiResource(operations: [
    new GetCollection(
        parameters: [
            'filter[minPrice]' => new QueryParameter(
                filter: new ComparisonFilter(new ExactFilter()),
                property: 'price',
                description: 'Minimum price'
            ),
        ]
    ),
])]
class Product {}
```

By analogy with the page's date examples, clients would send something like `?filter[minPrice][gte]=10`.

## Adding a parameter with a provider

Built-in providers are in `ApiPlatform\State\ParameterProvider`:

```php
<?php
// api/src/Resource/WithParameter.php
namespace App\Resource;

use ApiPlatform\Metadata\ApiResource;
use ApiPlatform\Metadata\Get;
use ApiPlatform\Metadata\QueryParameter;
use ApiPlatform\State\ParameterProvider\IriConverterParameterProvider;
use ApiPlatform\State\ParameterProvider\ReadLinkParameterProvider;
use App\Entity\Dummy;

#[ApiResource(operations: [
    new Get(
        uriTemplate: '/with_parameters_iris',
        parameters: [
            // Converts an IRI such as /dummies/1 into an entity
            'dummy' => new QueryParameter(
                provider: IriConverterParameterProvider::class,
                extraProperties: ['fetch_data' => true]
            ),
            // Resolves an identifier into a linked resource
            'owner' => new QueryParameter(
                provider: ReadLinkParameterProvider::class,
                extraProperties: ['resource_class' => Dummy::class]
            ),
        ]
    ),
])]
class WithParameter {}
```

A custom provider implements `ApiPlatform\State\ParameterProviderInterface`:

```php
<?php
// src/State/DynamicGroupProvider.php
namespace App\State;

use ApiPlatform\Metadata\Operation;
use ApiPlatform\Metadata\Parameter;
use ApiPlatform\State\ParameterProviderInterface;

final class DynamicGroupProvider implements ParameterProviderInterface
{
    public function provide(Parameter $parameter, array $parameters = [], array $context = []): ?Operation
    {
        return $context['operation'] ?? null;
    }
}
```

Attach it with `new QueryParameter(provider: DynamicGroupProvider::class)`.

## Sort parameters

`SortFilter` is the recommended option. `OrderFilter` is the legacy option.

```php
<?php
// api/src/Resource/Product.php
namespace App\Resource;

use ApiPlatform\Metadata\ApiResource;
use ApiPlatform\Metadata\GetCollection;
use ApiPlatform\Metadata\QueryParameter;
// SortFilter and OrderFilter namespaces are not shown on this page;
// check the Doctrine Filters documentation for the exact import.
use ApiPlatform\Doctrine\Orm\Filter\SortFilter;
use ApiPlatform\Doctrine\Orm\Filter\OrderFilter;

#[ApiResource(operations: [
    new GetCollection(
        parameters: [
            'sortName' => new QueryParameter(
                filter: new SortFilter(),
                property: 'name'
            ),
            'sortLegacy' => new QueryParameter(
                filter: OrderFilter::class,
                property: 'price'
            ),
        ]
    ),
])]
class Product {}
```

`SortFilter` supports dot notation for nested properties and a `nullsComparison` option. The page does not show the exact sort query syntax, so check the Doctrine Filters documentation for that.

